## The city of the vulture goddess

Nekhbet was a goddess already ancient when the pyramids were built: a white vulture with her wings spread over the king, the protector of the whole of Upper Egypt. Her city was Nekheb, today El Kab, on the east bank of the Nile between Luxor and Edfu. Its great mud-brick walls still stand around the site, and behind them a desert hill holds the tombs of the men who governed it.

Almost nobody comes. The cruise boats pass by on the river and the buses take the main road to Edfu, so you very often have the whole place to yourselves.

## A soldier tells his story

The tombs on the hill belong to the early New Kingdom, the age when Egypt threw out its foreign Hyksos rulers and began centuries of empire. In one of them, Ahmose, son of Ebana, a soldier from El Kab, had his life carved on the wall in his own words: the battles, the ships, the rewards from the king. Nearby, painted scenes show farmers ploughing and harvesting and families at a feast, still bright after some three and a half thousand years.

> A goddess's valley, a soldier's own words on the wall, and nobody else for miles.

## The temple in the empty valley

A short drive into the desert, a small temple built by Amenhotep III for Nekhbet and Hathor stands alone in a dry valley, with nothing around it but sand and low hills. It's the kind of place that stays with you because of the silence around it. On the Original day, your concierge takes you there before the heat and gives you all the time you want; on the Signature day, a licensed Egyptologist can join you to read the tombs and the temple.

## Left to us

El Kab is about an hour and a half south of Luxor. Opening the tombs, the timing and every arrangement on the day are left to your concierge, with local knowledge and our craft. With Esna on the same road, it makes a morning of goddesses and colour, or a quiet stop on our Temple Road day south to Aswan. Bring a hat; we'll bring the water and everything else.
